- fixed-sh-fails-under-non-mmap-build: `tests/fixed.sh` through a build without
  `HAVE_MMAP` stops early with a parse error and prints no summary: the script
  is then read through a real fd, and an earlier test in the file disturbs it
  (a comment line is parsed as code). Any prefix of the file parses fine on its
  own, so it depends on the descriptor state the earlier tests leave behind.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  cmake -S . -B /tmp/nm -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DHAVE_MMAP=OFF && cmake --build /tmp/nm -j
  /tmp/nm/shish tests/fixed.sh | tail -2   # tests/fixed.sh:1493:38: unexpected token (
  head -n 1500 tests/fixed.sh > /tmp/p.sh; /tmp/nm/shish /tmp/p.sh   # parses fine
  ```

- yash-suite-other-hangs: of the 119 `tests/yash/*.tst` files, only
  `while-y.tst` still hangs (killed by the per-file timeout, 20s here); the rest
  finish, several slowly: `typeset-y` ~19s, `redir-y` ~8s, `cmdprint-y` ~5s,
  `function-y` ~4s. So `DO_YASH_TESTS` stays off by default (see `TODO.md`).
  `while-y.tst` hangs on `while echo x; do done` (empty body, infinite loop; needs a
  POSIX mode, see `TODO.md` design decisions).

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  timeout 20 sh tests/run-tst.sh "$PWD/build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish" tests/yash while-y.tst; echo $?   # 124
  ```

- eval-lineno-imprecise-inside-function: `$LINENO` read from within an
  `eval`'d string takes the line of the invoking statement, but the value
  it inherits (`parse_lineno`, stamped once per top-level list by
  `sh_loop()`) is the *call site's* line, not the eval statement's own
  line when eval runs from inside a function or loop body whose
  definition spans multiple lines -- there is no per-command location
  tracking to fall back on for that case (unlike a direct, non-eval'd
  `$LINENO` reference, which reads the parse-time `loc` stamped
  individually on its own `N_ARGPARAM` node).

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  cat <<'EOF' | shish
  f() {
    echo $LINENO   # correctly prints 2
    eval 'echo $LINENO'   # prints 5 (f's call site), should print 3
  }
  f
  EOF
  ```

- set-notify-no-effect: POSIX `-b` is accepted (`set -b`, `set -o notify`,
  shown in `$-`) but changes nothing: a finished background job is still
  reported only just before the next prompt. Needs a real "OK to interrupt
  terminal output" toggle in `src/job/job_clean.c`'s banner path, which
  currently assumes deferral is always correct.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  printf 'set -b\nsleep 0.1 &\nsleep 1\n' | shish -i 2>&1
  # the "Done" banner should appear as soon as the job ends, not after sleep 1
  ```

- set-verbose-partial: `set -v` echoes each line the top-level script, stdin or `-c`
  string supplies to stderr, but not a final line without a newline (`shish -v -c
  'echo a'` prints no echo), not text read by `.`/`eval`, and a `\`-continued line
  is echoed joined.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  shish -v -c 'echo a'      # no "echo a" on stderr; bash echoes it
  ```

- set-histexpand-unimplemented: `struct shopt`'s `histexpand` bit
  (`src/sh.h`, `-H`) is set correctly by `set -H`/`set +H` and
  reflected back correctly in `$-`, but nothing checks it either --
  more fundamentally, there is no history-expansion ('!') feature
  implemented at all under `src/history/` or `src/term/` for this
  flag to gate. It's a placeholder with nothing behind it.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  echo 'set -H; echo $-' | shish
  # shish accepts -H and sets the bit, but ! expansion doesn't work
  # (no history expansion feature exists yet)
  ```

- ubsan-buffer-op-proto-function-type-mismatch: `lib/buffer.h`'s
  `buffer_op_proto` is declared as `ssize_t(int, void*, size_t,
  void*)`. `read`/`write` (external libc, `int(int,void*,size_t)`) are
  cast to `buffer_op_proto*` throughout `src/fd/`, `src/fdtable/`,
  `src/history/`, and `src/sh/` -- UB by the letter of the standard,
  but `-fsanitize=function` never flags it: it embeds its type-
  descriptor comparison only in functions the sanitizer itself
  compiled, and libc's `read`/`write` weren't. Not planned to be
  "fixed", since a fix here would mean wrapping two libc functions
  everywhere they're used for a change with no observable effect
  under any sanitizer or real ABI this project targets.

  Two more real mismatches exist, in `lib/buffer/buffer_tosa.c`
  (`strallocwrite`, `char*` where `buffer_op_proto` wants `void*`) and
  `lib/buffer/buffer_frombuf.c` (`buffer_dummyreadbuf`, `fd_t` where
  it wants `int` -- identical on every platform this project builds
  for, but not the same declared type) -- both dead code from shish's
  own perspective (`buffer_tosa()`/`buffer_frombuf()`, the functions
  that would ever install them as a callback, are never called
  anywhere under `src/`; they only exist because `lib/`'s whole-
  directory glob build compiles every libowfat source file, used or
  not). Left alone rather than "fixed" per this repo's own standing
  preference for not touching unused `lib/` code unasked.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  # This is a latent UB issue, not a runtime-visible bug.
  # Build with -fsanitize=function and run any shish command:
  cmake -B build/debug -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
    -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-fsanitize=function,undefined"
  cmake --build build/debug
  ./build/debug/shish -c 'echo hello'
  # No error reported (function sanitizer doesn't catch libc mismatches)
  ```

- asan-leak-residue-not-fully-triaged: ASan leak reports left under the
  `-fsanitize=address,undefined` build:

  - Parsing a function definition still leaks 2 allocations (a 56-byte
    `N_ARGSTR` from `parse_pipeline.c:13`'s `parse_gettok()` plus its
    string); only visible when it is parsed repeatedly:
    `i=0; while [ $i -lt 50 ]; do eval "f(){ echo a; }"; i=$((i+1)); done`
    leaks 2 allocations per pass.
  - The installed function list (`eval_function.c`) and the last copy of
    each variable's value live for the whole process. The value copy is
    not marked `V_FREESTR`, because `var_setvsa()` reads that flag as "the
    offset is the start of the value" and would corrupt it.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  cmake -B build/asan -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
    -DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="-fsanitize=address,undefined"
  cmake --build build/asan
  ASAN_OPTIONS=log_path=lk ./build/asan/shish tests/fixed.sh
  cat lk.* | grep -A6 'leak of'
  ```

- signal-tests-vary-with-machine-load: the `sig*-p` files are timing
  sensitive enough that the same binary produces wildly different
  scores from one run to the next. `sigterm1-p.tst` scored 36/180 in
  one `ctest` run and 177/180 in the next; `sigterm6-p.tst` 89 then
  125; `sigcont1-p.tst` 148 then 177. The low scores all came from
  runs made while the machine was busy (parallel builds, other test
  runs); the high ones from an otherwise idle machine. Standalone runs
  agree with the idle `ctest` numbers. Until this is understood, any
  `sig*` measurement needs an idle machine and a second run to
  confirm. Probably the tests' own `kill`-then-observe races rather
  than a shell bug, but that is an assumption, not a finding.

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  # run this twice, once while a "cmake --build -j8" is running
  sh tests/run-tst.sh "$PWD/build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish" tests/posix sigterm1-p.tst
  ```

- alias-substitution-remaining-cases: alias substitution now happens in
  `parse_gettok()` (`alias-p.tst` 62/65). Still open:
  - `:430` an alias that expands to `()` after a function name
    (`alias f='f ' p='()'; f p`): the `name (` test in
    `parse_command.c` looks at the raw next character, not at aliases.
  - `:548` an alias whose text ends in `\` does not continue onto the
    newline behind it (same reason).
  - `:576` a command substitution is parsed when it is expanded, not when
    the enclosing function is defined, so an alias set in the function
    body is not seen by `$(...)` parsed earlier.

- quote-backslash-escaping-broken: `quote-p.tst` is 34/35. Open:
  `:431` an alias is expanded inside a command substitution in double
  quotes (dash does the same; see `TODO.md` design decisions).

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  sh tests/run-tst.sh "$PWD/build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish" tests/posix quote-p.tst
  ```

- input-not-read-line-wise: inside a command substitution the shell reads more
  input than needed and consumes data meant for the commands after it
  (`input-p.tst:89`, "shell input is line-wise (command substitution)"; 10/11).

  **Repro:**
  ```sh
  sh tests/run-tst.sh "$PWD/build/x86_64-linux-gnu/shish" tests/posix input-p.tst
  ```

- grouping-p-tst-flaky: `grouping-p.tst:34` ("subshell ending with
  asynchronous list", `(echo foo >fifo1&); cat fifo1`) fails
  intermittently (2 of 12 runs, `grouping-p.tst: 11/12 passed`): some race
